    description: "¡Que Madre! Media is an initiative of the Youth Leadership Institute that serves young people of color, ages 15 to 25, in the Eastern Coachella Valley (ECV). The program provides a platform for participants to address mental health challenges—including depression, anxiety, and relationship issues—through storytelling and community advocacy. The program's approach to mental health care is holistic, emphasizing community care practices, and involves collaborations with culturally competent mental health experts and providers. While the program welcomes all young people aged 15-25 in the ECV, it primarily serves young women of color. Program participants regularly produce multimedia content that focuses on intersectional community issues, including mental health, school climate, and community justice. This youth-created content is disseminated online via the program’s website and Instagram, and is also included in regularly published print magazines. Additionally, ¡Que Madre! engages in direct advocacy on school climate through its involvement in the Alianza Community Justice Campaign (formerly the Education Justice campaign), which the program joined in 2020. This advocacy supports the Local Control and Accountability Plan (LCAP) to ensure school funds align with youth priorities, such as the implementation of restorative justice practices. These efforts contributed to the allocation of funds for Wellness Centers across the four high schools in the Coachella Valley Unified School District (CVUSD) in 2022. The program includes the Youth Mental Health Fellowship (2025-2028), which is designed to investigate and advocate for effective delivery of the Behavioral Health Services Act (BHSA). This fellowship will recruit and train seven youth leaders who will conduct community-based research to understand youth experiences with behavioral health services. Fellows will then meet with local and state legislators to advocate for policy changes to improve access to mental health services. Overall, ¡Que Madre! Media equips participants with advocacy skills, promotes mental health awareness, and fosters community building within the Eastern Coachella Valley.",
