    description: "The Senate of Virginia Page Program is a dynamic educational experience aimed at fostering the next generation of civic leaders, administrators, and elected officials. This program has a long history, dating back to at least 1850, and is structured to provide a comprehensive foundation in civic responsibility and the legislative process. Participants, known as Senate Pages, engage in various duties such as staffing committee meetings, answering phone calls, and providing concierge services, all while maintaining high standards of conduct. The program curriculum is divided into three main components: The Responsible Young Professional, The Evolving Leader, and The Civic-Minded Young Adult. Senate Pages are tasked with job assignments that emphasize service and professional development, including leadership and decision-making skills, etiquette, money management, and current events. They also participate in community service projects in partnership with local non-profits. The capstone of the program is a mock legislative session where Pages assume the roles of legislators, debating and voting on topics. The program is delivered in-person during the Virginia General Assembly's legislative session, which convenes in January. Depending on the year, this session lasts either 46 or 60 days. Participants reside in a hotel and are required to report each Sunday evening, departing each Friday at noon. Eligibility is restricted to 13 or 14-year-olds by January who are residents of Virginia, with applications reviewed and decisions announced in mid-December. Graduates of the program are well-prepared for further leadership opportunities in high school and college programs.",
