    description: "The Scientific Enrichment Month Program is a free virtual program provided by the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center at Columbia University. This program is designed for high school students and takes place in July, focusing on cancer education, scientific enrichment, and professional development. The main objective of this program is to provide students with an understanding of various aspects of cancer research, cancer health inequities, and public health. The curriculum includes lectures on how to develop a hypothesis, understanding cancer research, and addressing cancer health inequities. Students will also receive professional development lessons in resume writing, personal statements, interviewing skills, and public speaking. A selection of past topics includes an introduction to cancer research, cancer health equity, environmental equity, cancer prevention and risk reduction through behavior changes, and insights from cancer researchers. The Scientific Enrichment Month Program is delivered online via Zoom sessions, held on Mondays, Tuesdays, and Wednesdays from 4:00 to 5:30 PM (EST) throughout the month of July. Registration for this program typically opens in early March and closes in late June or when the program reaches its capacity. The program is led by Jasmine A. McDonald, Ph.D., and Mary Beth Terry, Ph.D., who serve as Co-Directors of the YES in THE HEIGHTS program at the Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center. Both Dr. McDonald and Dr. Terry have extensive expertise in epidemiology. Any interested high school students can participate. Upon completion, students have the opportunity to earn an HICCC Certificate, provided they participate in a specified number of sessions across the month. This indicates that participants will gain a deeper understanding of cancer research, health inequities, and key professional skills.",
