    description: "Bikes Not Bombs offers a three-year Youth Apprenticeship program designed to place graduates of their Bike School in various work roles, with the aim of building critical workplace skills and developing post-secondary plans for economic mobility. The apprenticeship focuses on three areas: Bike Recycling and International Shipping, Bike School, and Bike Shop. In the Bike Recycling and International Shipping Apprenticeship, participants learn all aspects of bicycle recycling, including collections, bicycle and parts sorting, and distribution. It is a crucial part of the organization's operations, and with the help of Youth Apprentices, Bikes Not Bombs has distributed over 80,000 bicycles to international partners. The Bike School Apprenticeship trains program alumni to teach mechanics lessons, lead group rides, and engage participants in conversations and campaigns around social justice. Apprentices will gain valuable experience in planning and implementing the curriculum for four Bike School programs: Bike Institute, Earn-A-Bike, On-The-Bike, and Sisters In Action. Within the Bike Shop Apprenticeship, participants master bike part analysis, inventory management, bike mechanics and refurbishment, and sales and customer service competencies. Apprentices work with donated bicycles that are processed through the recycling program, becoming proficient mechanics by the end of their apprenticeship. For eligibility, applicants must be aged 14-18 during the employment period, be a full-time resident of the City of Boston, and be legally permitted to work in the United States. Further, Bikes Not Bombs also offers a Bicyclists Organizing for Community Action (BOCA) Apprenticeship, where Youth Apprentices lead and organize members to foster healthy communities and fight for youth rights, jobs, economic, racial, and environmental, and transit justice.",
