    description: "The Healthcare Career Exploration Program (HCEP) at Brigham and Women's Hospital is an immersive, in-person experience aimed at high school students interested in exploring careers in healthcare. The program is designed to offer students a comprehensive understanding of various healthcare roles through direct interaction with professionals in a hospital setting. Over the course of seven weeks, participants engage in career exploration sessions that are designed to provide a well-rounded perspective on healthcare careers, particularly for those who are uncertain about their future college majors or who have been discouraged from pursuing a healthcare path. The curriculum includes seven 90-minute sessions with healthcare professionals across different specialties, held weekly on Thursdays. Students also participate in 1-2 weekly volunteer shifts, which occur on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, or Friday. The volunteer component includes Central Transport Services shifts in July and Patient Access Services shifts in August, along with a 1-hour Patient Access Services training session. The program concludes with a finale session, where participants receive a certificate of completion and a letter of recommendation. To be eligible for HCEP, participants must be rising high school juniors or seniors, or 2025 high school graduates. They must be at least 16 years old and able to attend at least six of the seven weeks of the program, totaling a minimum of 22 hours. Additionally, students must be local to Boston or able to commute for weekly shifts. The program runs from early July to late August, with mandatory orientation and welcome sessions scheduled before the start of the program. Through this experience, students gain insights into the healthcare field and have the opportunity to continue volunteering during the school year.",
