    description: "Nā Manu ʻElele, also known as the Hawaiʻi Land Steward Program, is a collaborative initiative between Kupu and the State of Hawaiʻi’s Department of Land and Natural Resources (DLNR). The program's main objective is to provide temporary work and training opportunities for local “stewards” who assist in interpreting the historic nature, natural history, and safety concerns of sensitive natural areas. The program aims to mitigate unwanted behaviors and ensure safety and empowers local communities to manage and mitigate high visitor use in sensitive heritage areas. The program will take place on the islands of Hawaiʻi, Maui, Oʻahu, and Kauaʻi, and a total of twenty-four stewards will be placed across these sites in part-time or full-time positions. The program usually starts in mid February 12, 2023, with an application deadline of December 15, 2023, however, applications will remain open until all positions are filled. Interested applicants must be over the age of 17, be a US Citizen, US National, US Permanent Resident, or otherwise legally allowed to work in the United States. They must also be responsible for their own personal housing and transportation arrangements on-island. Participants in the Nā Manu ʻElele Land Steward Program can expect to gain a deeper understanding of the historic nature and natural history of sensitive heritage areas in Hawaiʻi. They will also develop skills in managing visitor behavior and ensuring safety in these areas. Participants will receive a wage of $18/hr paid biweekly and may be eligible for health coverage if working 20+ hrs/week for 4 consecutive weeks.",
