    description: "The Intergenerational Chess Internship provided by The Dorot Foundation is a unique program designed to foster meaningful intergenerational relationships through the game of chess. The primary objective is to create a setting where wisdom and curiosity can meet face-to-face, with participants from both high school students in grades 6 - 12 and adults aged 65 and above, teaching and learning chess from one another. The program operates on a weekly basis during the academic year, specifically on Monday afternoons from 4:30-6:00 PM. While the main focus is on the game of chess, the curriculum also emphasizes the exchange of stories and experiences, fostering a deeper understanding and connection between generations. Through these shared experiences, participants not only strengthen their chess-playing skills but also gain valuable insights from different generational perspectives. The program is conducted in-person at DOROT's premises on 171 West 85th Street. A group interview and an application are the key prerequisites to participating in the program. The Fall 2023 dates indicate that the program runs from mid-October to early December, although it's important to note the specific dates may vary in different years. The program was established by Zachary Targoff and his parents, who were inspired by the bond Zachary developed with Herman Bomze, a senior participant in the program. However, specific details about the current instructors and their qualifications are not provided. Upon completion of the program, participants can expect to have improved their chess skills and formed impactful, meaningful intergenerational relationships. While no specific certifications are awarded, the knowledge and skills gained in interpersonal communication and understanding across generations are invaluable outcomes.",
