    description: "The Institute for College Preparation (ICP) at Georgetown University is an intensive pre-college academic enrichment program, targeted at students from selected D.C. public schools. It aims to explore significant historical and current issues related to urban injustice, racial relations, and immigration in the U.S., while also defining the career opportunities that a college education can provide. Students are introduced to cultural heritage studies, notions of privileged nationalities, and an international world-view of racism and inequality. The program encourages students to reflect on their own experiences with urban equity and inequity, increasing their self-confidence and reinforcing personal and social responsibility. The ICP program runs from the 7th grade through to high school graduation, with a carefully developed curriculum delivered via Saturday Academy classes and a Summer Institute on Georgetown University’s campus. The Saturday Academy focuses on core subjects such as English, Math, and Spanish, whereas the Summer Institute concentrates on Science, Math, Spanish, English, and an elective. During the summer course, 11th-grade students live on Georgetown University’s campus, providing a taste of life on a college campus. The ICP program is coordinated by Sofia Alsua, a graduate from the Engaged and Public Humanities Master’s program, who focuses on transforming higher education structures to be more equitable and sustainable. The faculty advisor is Elizabeth Velez, a retired English Professor who has been with the Community Scholars Program for over two decades. Students participating in the ICP program are expected to reflect on values that form their identities and to assume roles as responsible world citizens. They will gain a deeper understanding and awareness of social issues and develop effectiveness in critical thinking, test-taking, and study skills. They will also expand their college and career pathways, and broaden their networks in pursuing change for the greater good.",
