    description: "The OMRF Teen Leaders Program offered by the Oklahoma Medical Research Foundation (OMRF) is a leadership program aimed at cultivating the next generation of leaders in medical and research fields. The program is open to Oklahoma high school students who are interested in gaining a deeper understanding of community support, leadership skills, and the workings of OMRF. It provides participants with a thorough understanding of the nonprofit sector, research centers, and the processes of medical research. Students also gain insights into careers in biomedical research and clinical work through tours of OMRF labs, meetings with scientific leaders, and hands-on experiences. The program takes place in person at OMRF, with sessions typically held from 1:30-4 p.m. on the second Tuesday of each month from September to April, excluding December. A unique feature of the program is the development of an advocacy and fundraising project in the spring, supervised and assisted by OMRF Philanthropy staff. To be eligible for the program, students must be in their sophomore, junior, or senior year of high school during the program year, be in good standing at their school, and attend a school in Oklahoma. The program does not have a specific GPA requirement and is free to participants, funded by OMRF. However, students are responsible for their own transportation to the sessions and/or events. The program culminates in a celebration event for the students and their families. Successful participants will leave the program with developed leadership skills, a deeper understanding of medical research and the nonprofit sector, and the experience of having executed a community advocacy and fundraising project.",
