    description: "One Day Debate Tournaments provided by the Bella Abzug Leadership Institute (BALI) aim to develop debate skills among middle and high school students, especially those from underserved communities. These one-day events, held twice a year, incorporate debate into the larger goal of preparing youth for college, career, and civic engagement. The main objective is to equip students with critical thinking, public speaking, and argumentation skills through structured debate competitions. The curriculum includes a morning session featuring a speaker on a contemporary debate topic, followed by team preparations for oral arguments and written materials. The afternoon is dedicated to the debate competition where teams of two girls debate against another team of two. Topics chosen are often relevant to current societal issues, such as the resolution for one event being \"The United States should replace means tested welfare with a Universal Basic Income.\" The key skills covered include critical thinking, public speaking, research, and teamwork. These tournaments are delivered in-person, typically held at venues like the Silberman School of Social Work at Hunter College. The event runs from 9:30 AM to 3:00 PM, including breakfast, lunch, and an awards ceremony. The instructor for the morning session includes notable speakers like Almaz Zelleke, a political science professor with expertise in political theory and public policy. Participants do not need previous debate experience, and the tournaments are open to students from 6th to 12th grade attending any NYC public school. The main outcomes and benefits of participating in these tournaments include enhanced debate and public speaking skills, a deeper understanding of contemporary issues, and the ability to construct and present arguments effectively. Participants also gain experience in teamwork and collaboration, which are valuable for their academic and professional futures.",
