    description: "The QuarkNet Workshop, hosted by the Physics Division at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, is an in-person learning opportunity designed for high school physics teachers and students. This workshop aims to create a vibrant learning community focused on cutting-edge research in particle physics and cosmology. Participants will have the chance to engage with presentations from Berkeley Lab scientists and postdocs, providing an intense overview of current scientific inquiries in these fields. The workshop curriculum covers a range of advanced topics, including the Higgs boson, research with the ATLAS experiment at CERN, neutrino physics, the search for dark matter, and mapping the universe. Additional key subjects include measuring dark energy with supernovae and applications of machine learning in physics research. Participants will also tour research facilities at the lab, enhancing their understanding of the scientific process and research applications. This free workshop takes place over several days in June for students, with an additional day for teachers. Esteemed presentations, such as those by Nobel Laureate Saul Perlmutter and George Smoot on CMB cosmology, are highlights of the program. There are no costs involved for teachers or students to attend, making it accessible to a wide audience.",
