    description: "The Teen Network Board (TNB) is a county and school board-appointed advocacy group in Arlington County, designed for high school and home school students from grades 9-12. It comprises fifty students from diverse backgrounds and areas within the county. The main objective of the TNB is to provide a community voice for Arlington youth and families, discuss issues, make recommendations, and act to improve the lives of Arlington teens. The program is delivered in-person, with meetings held on the 1st and 3rd Tuesday of each month during the school year from 6:30-8 pm. The TNB is structured into four subcommittees that focus on different areas of community improvement. These include the Drugs and Alcohol Prevention (DAP) subcommittee, which aims to prevent substance and alcohol use and support mental health among Arlington youth. The Healthy Relationships Taskforce (HRT) promotes healthy and consensual relationship behaviors while advocating for the prevention of intimate partner and sexual violence. The Peer to Peer (P2P) subcommittee creates programming and events offering peer support and mentorship, including opportunities for mental health support. Lastly, the Volunteering, Internships, and Community Engagement (VICE) subcommittee connects teens with community service, internship, and volunteer opportunities. The program is open to any youth in grades 9-12 residing in Arlington County, with no specific prerequisites for participation other than residency and grade level. Through engagement in the TNB, participants gain skills in advocacy, community service, and leadership, while also contributing to the wellbeing and improvement of their community.",
