    description: "The Conservation Leadership Development Program (CLDP) by Kupu is a paid, entry-level initiative designed for early professionals aged 17 and above who are interested in pursuing a career in conservation. It matches program members with a Host Site that best suits their conservation interests. The CLDP provides an in-depth, entry-level environmental experience from an agency of interest, thereby helping protect the Pacific Islands and Hawaii’s natural resources and making a positive impact in the community. The program offers opportunities in various fields including ornithology, botany, natural resource management, aquatic resource management, marine biology, Hawaiian cultural studies, and agricultural science. It is based in the Hawaiian Islands and U.S. Pacific Islands and can be undertaken for a duration of either 6 months or up to 1 year, on a full-time basis with a 40 hours per week commitment. The CLDP is facilitated by mentors who provide supervision for members and the delivery involves performing meaningful direct service in conservation that addresses local environmental needs and makes an impact on the people and community. The participants are expected to be comfortable serving in outdoor environments and perform physically demanding duties. In terms of prerequisites, participants should be at least 17 years old by the program start date, possess or be working towards a high school diploma or equivalent, have their own housing and transportation, and pass a criminal history check. On successful completion of the program, participants receive a Segal AmeriCorps Education Award. The amount of the award depends upon the term of service, with awards of $3,447.50 for half-time term members and $6,895.00 for full-time term members. The program helps participants develop leadership skills, compassion towards the environment, and respect for the cultural heritage of the land, thus paving the way for future employment opportunities in conservation.",
