    url: "https://www.kupuhawaii.org/kupu-community-program",
    description: "The Kupu Community Program, based in Oahu, is a year-long initiative designed to prepare under-resourced youth for the workforce. The program offers life skills training, educational opportunities, and green job prospects. Initially, participants engage in a 6-week phase of Mālama ʻĀina, guided by knowledgeable staff. Upon completion, they move onto their educational objectives. In partnership with the Department of Education schools in Honolulu District, qualifying students can work towards their high school diploma. For others, the program collaborates with McKinley School for Adults, providing tutoring and assistance with HiSet/GED testing. In addition to academic support, Kupu Community Program includes Workforce Development through a culinary and events program. The program is conducted at the Hoʻokupu Center in Kewalo Basin Harbor, Honolulu, and applications are accepted throughout the year. The program aims to rehabilitate Hawaii's ecosystems, increase sustainability, and offer participants the chance to serve their community while gaining vocational and life skills. Participants also have the opportunity to obtain a Certificate of High School Equivalency and are eligible for paid on-the-job training placements or financial assistance for vocational training costs. To be eligible for this program, participants must be between the ages of 16-24, have reliable transportation to Kewalo Basin, and be interested in natural resource conservation, renewable energy, or sustainable living. They should also meet the necessary physical and behavioral requirements. Through participation in the Kupu Community Program, individuals will gain real-world job experience, enhance personal development, and have the opportunity to engage in cultural activities.",
