    url: "https://projectengages.gatech.edu/",
    description: "Project ENGAGES is a high school science education program developed by Georgia Tech, in partnership with seven minority-serving public high schools in the City of Atlanta. This program primarily serves African-American students, many of whom receive free or subsidized lunch. The program consists of three main components: Science Outreach Initiative, Research Experiences for Students, and Science Training for Teachers. The Science Outreach Initiative aims to provide in-depth exposure to science for students through direct interactions with Georgia Tech researchers and laboratories, high bandwidth video conferencing, and the establishment of science clubs. The Research Experience for Students component allows students to conduct their own research projects under the mentorship of an engineering or science researcher. These research experiences extend for a 12-month period, during which students work full-time in the summer and 15 hours per week during the school year. Prior to undertaking their own research, students participate in a 4-week summer \"Boot Camp\" to learn the necessary scientific research skills. The student's completed project will then be presented at a science fair competition. The Science Training for Teachers component allows science teachers from partner schools to gain exposure to engineering and science research and acquire laboratory skills by training with a Georgia Tech research scientist. This equips teachers to expand their classroom curriculum into new areas of science and engineering, thereby having a wider impact on science education in the partnering high schools. Project ENGAGES is co-directed by Professors Tequila A. L. Harris and Edward Botchwey, with the assistance of Lakeita Servance, Assistant Director of Outreach Initiatives, and Michael J. Glenn, Ed.D., Program and Operations Manager, Outreach K-20 Programs. The program is open to high school students with an interest in science, biology, bioengineering, biotechnology, physics, and other areas of engineering, such as environmental engineering, materials, and robotics. Interested students are expected to spend a significant amount of time in their Georgia Tech research laboratory and participate in enrichment programs.",
