    description: "The Schomburg Center Junior Scholars Program, provided by the New York Public Library, is a transformative educational initiative that encourages historical literacy through a variety of academic and creative activities. Each year, the program engages 100 youth from grades 6 through 12 in an intensive study of the center’s archives and resources. The curriculum is dynamic, with themes changing annually in relation to the Schomburg Center’s lead exhibitions, archival and digital collections, and current events. The Junior Scholars Program emphasizes the significant contributions of African-Americans to U.S. history and culture and provides a deeper understanding of the participants' heritage. The program is delivered in-person and follows a tuition-free Saturday schedule during the academic year from 10 AM to 3 PM. It adopts various instructional methods such as college-style lectures and presentations, group discussions, and project-based learning. Participants engage in individual research, original writing, art portfolios, and collaborative multimedia arts projects. The curriculum features activities like lectures/seminars, video and film screenings, cultural arts performances, special workshops in various artistic disciplines, research projects, professional development forums, educational tours of historical and cultural sites in New York City, and “Teen Talk” book discussion groups. Upon completion of the program, Junior Scholars gain an expanded knowledge of their intellectual, social, cultural, and artistic identities. They embrace their legacy as African-American citizens and learn about the cultures of their prolific and trailblazing ancestors. For application, the priority deadline typically falls in late July, with the final deadline in late August. Group interviews, which are by invitation, take place in September and October.",
