    description: "The NASA Glenn High School Engineering Institute is a free, week-long program held at the NASA Glenn Research Center in Cleveland, designed for high school students interested in pursuing engineering careers in the aerospace industry. The institute's primary objective is to introduce students to engineering and technology concepts used by NASA STEM professionals to achieve research and development goals. Through this program, students utilize the Engineering Design Process to design and test prototypes in key areas such as acoustic damping, power management and distribution, and simulated lunar operations. These skills are intended to be applicable to students' future academic and career pursuits in STEM fields. The curriculum involves hands-on projects where students engage in the following activities: - Designing and testing power systems for future space stations that utilize solar and battery power, with features for power re-routing and automated shutdowns. - Developing and fabricating non-inflatable wheels for lunar environments, tested on specific terrain conditions. - Creating and testing acoustic liners to reduce noise pollution from jet engines using additive manufacturing techniques. The program is conducted in-person at the research center over three sessions in July, each accommodating 20 students. Students must cover their own meals, lodging, and transportation. The application process involves an online form and a recommendation letter, with selections being announced in mid-May. Eligibility is restricted to U.S. citizens or lawful permanent residents who will be high school juniors or seniors in the upcoming academic year, have a minimum GPA of 3.2, and demonstrate a strong interest in engineering careers at NASA. Participants are expected to gain fundamental STEM skills and practical experience that align with NASA's future workforce needs, preparing them for potential careers in the aerospace industry.",
