    description: "The Chicago Police and Firefighter Training Academy (CPFTA) is a two-year training program designed for high school students interested in careers in public safety, law enforcement, criminal justice, and fire science. This unique program was developed collaboratively by the Chicago Public Schools’ Department of Career and Technical Education, the Chicago Police Department, the Chicago Fire Department, and the City Colleges of Chicago. It aims to recruit and train up to 125 public and non-public high school students each year, with the goal of providing a pool of qualified applicants for the city’s police and fire academies. The CPFTA curriculum allows participants to progress seamlessly from high school directly into any City College of Chicago to complete their associate’s degree. Cadets also have the opportunity to earn OSHA, Cyber Security Safety, Unarmed Security Guard (PERC), and even the EMT-B state licensure for State Certification. Furthermore, the program includes a possible paid summer internship between the junior and senior year, allowing students to gain a deeper understanding of all aspects of careers in public safety. The CPFTA program is delivered in-person at the Chicago Police Academy or the Chicago Fire Academy, with classes held two days a week (Tuesday and Wednesday) from 4:30 p.m. to 7:30 p.m. Each semester, junior and senior students rotate between instruction at these two academies. In order to participate in the CPFTA program, one must be a Chicago resident, a high school sophomore, and have a GPA of 2.3 or better upon completing the first two years of high school, along with 90% attendance or better. Other prerequisites include two letters of recommendation, a valid school ID, state ID, or valid driver's license with a good driving record, a current physical examination, and successful completion of a background check, which includes fingerprinting and no felony arrests, convictions, or gang affiliations. Upon completion of the two years in high school and two years at a city college, students will have met the minimum educational requirements to take the Chicago Police and Fire Department examinations for entry into their respective academies, setting them on a clear path towards a career in public safety.",
