    description: "Work to Ride is a non-profit, community-based prevention program established in 1994, with a mission to help Philadelphia’s under-resourced youth through activities centered on horsemanship, equine sports, and education. The program provides a unique opportunity for young individuals to interact with animals and nature, promoting discipline, self-esteem, motivation, social development, life skills, academic achievement, and physical fitness. The program is designed as a long-term commitment for youths aged 7 to 18, with the expectation that participants will remain with the program through high school graduation. Participants develop discipline, responsibility, and life skills through training, riding, and performing horse care and barn management chores. The youth are allowed to choose among various horse sports, with polo being a popular choice. The program also emphasizes the importance of academic achievement, with staff and volunteers providing after-school tutoring to help students maintain passing grades required for program participation. Work to Ride's programs and activities are designed to engage youth in educational, social, and cultural experiences. Participants build relationships and develop problem-solving skills through spirited teamwork and cooperation. Not only does the program provide vocational guidance and opportunities for careers in related fields, but it also helps in developing family-like relationships that become integral components of their lifestyles. The Work to Ride program is housed at Chamounix Equestrian Center, located in West Fairmount Park, Philadelphia. The program involves physical participation, with activities at the stables as well as during competitions and trips. Moreover, Work to Ride graduates receive help with college enrollment and often return to Chamounix to mentor new participants.",
