    description: "The YES in THE HEIGHTS program, offered by Columbia University's Herbert Irving Comprehensive Cancer Center, is a summer internship that aims to increase the number of under-represented and under-resourced youth entering STEM fields. The program's mission is to reduce the cancer burden and health inequities through training and mentorship, with a focus on cancer. Over two years, the YES Student Equity Scholars gain practical experience in cancer research by working alongside renowned researchers and physicians. The program lasts between 8 to 12 weeks and includes comprehensive training such as a formal orientation, hands-on scientific training, academic etiquette, and professionalism. The curriculum also requires the completion of an Individual Development Plan and the submission of a scientific abstract of their work. Second-year students are required to make a scientific oral presentation. To be eligible for the program, students must be U.S. citizens or permanent residents, part of an underrepresented racial and/or ethnic minority group, first-generation college students, or economically disadvantaged. They must also display a high aptitude and interest in science and be in good standing at their current school. The program is led by Jasmine A. McDonald, an Assistant Professor of Epidemiology, and Mary Beth Terry, a Professor of Epidemiology. Their faculty includes specialists in Population Science, Clinical Science, and Basic Science. These professionals bring their expertise to the program, providing students with high-quality mentorship and education. The program is primarily in-person and takes place during the summer. The program provides a unique opportunity to delve into the world of cancer research, gain hands-on experience, and learn from leading professionals in the field. The skills and knowledge gained from this program would be invaluable for any student interested in pursuing a career in biomedical research, particularly in the field of cancer.",
